Require:

  len(decoded Transactions) == nTx

before returning a complete decoded block.

A malformed/incomplete RPC structure must return an error rather than
constructing a chain.Block that Store would later reject.


5. TXID / WTXID
===============

This boundary must preserve the finalized identity model exactly.

Core verbose transaction RPC:

  "txid"
      -> chain.Transaction.TxID

  "hash"
      -> chain.Transaction.WTxID

Do NOT derive either hash locally.
Do NOT substitute one for the other.

Map:

  version  -> uint32 Version
  locktime -> uint32 LockTime
  size
  vsize
  weight

Require lowercase 64-character hex hashes before handing them to Store, or
normalize only if Core can legitimately emit uppercase — do not accept
arbitrary malformed identity strings.

For a non-witness transaction Core should give:

  hash == txid

For a witnessed transaction:

  hash != txid

Store already enforces that identity relationship against actual witness
presence; the decoder should preserve Core data rather than recompute it.


6. INPUT DECODING
=================

COINBASE
--------

When RPC vin contains "coinbase":

  PreviousOut = nil
  Coinbase    = exact hex-decoded coinbase bytes
  ScriptSig   = nil/empty
  Sequence    = RPC sequence
  Witness     = txinwitness, if supplied

Do not put RPC coinbase bytes into ScriptSig.

ORDINARY INPUT
--------------

  PreviousOut.TxID  = vin.txid
  PreviousOut.Index = vin.vout

  Coinbase  = nil
  ScriptSig = exact decoded scriptSig.hex
  Sequence  = RPC sequence
  Witness   = txinwitness

Pure-witness inputs legitimately have:

  ScriptSig == empty

Preserve that.

WITNESS
-------

Every txinwitness entry is a hex string.

Decode every item independently to raw []byte.

Preserve:

- vin association
- item ordering
- zero-length witness items
- arbitrary binary values
- full item length

Never concatenate witness items.
Never truncate witness items.

A 17,088-byte item must survive this boundary exactly.


7. OUTPUT DECODING AND SCRIPT CLASSIFICATION
============================================

For every vout:

- decode value exactly to chain.Amount
- preserve n as Output.Index
- hex-decode scriptPubKey.hex exactly
- call:

    script.Classify(rawScript)

and populate from the classifier result:

  ScriptType
  WitnessVersion
  WitnessProgram
  PubKeys

The RPC:

  scriptPubKey.type

must NOT determine ScriptType.

It may be retained in the raw RPC DTO for diagnostics only.

Especially:

Core currently reports a P2QPK v2/32 output as:

  type = "witness_unknown"

The explorer must structurally classify that same raw script as:

  script.TypeP2QPK

Do not classify all witness_unknown outputs as P2QPK.


8. CORE-REPORTED ADDRESS HANDLING
=================================

For ordinary addressable scripts, copy Core's:

  scriptPubKey.address

as-is into:

  chain.Output.Address

where Core supplied it.

This includes the generic witness-unknown address Core already provides for
a structurally-P2QPK v2/32 output.

Therefore:

P2QPK:

  classification:
    structural v2/32 from script.Classify

  address:
    Core RPC address field as-is

Do NOT implement a Bech32m encoder for P2QPK.

Do NOT derive a P2QPK address from its public key.

The output program is:

  HASH256(pubkey)

not the raw public key.


9. P2PK ADDRESS RESOLUTION
==========================

Core deliberately does not include an address field for bare P2PK outputs.

The architecture already defines the Core-validated fallback:

Given classifier-extracted public key:

  pkh(<pubkey hex>)

Call:

  getdescriptorinfo("pkh(<pubkey hex>)")

Use the returned canonical descriptor/checksum with:

  deriveaddresses(<canonical descriptor>)

Expect exactly one address.

Store that address in:

  chain.Output.Address

while keeping:

  ScriptType = p2pk

Do not relabel the output as P2PKH.

This lets early QOGE P2PK coinbase outputs participate in address accounting
without lying about their script type.

Add a small address resolver abstraction/interface so decoder unit tests can
use a deterministic fake rather than requiring qogecoind.

The real resolver should use Core RPC.

Memoize resolved pubkeys in-memory for the lifetime of the resolver to avoid
repeating descriptor RPC calls for the same public key.

Do NOT hardcode QOGE Base58 prefixes in this decoder.


10. BARE MULTISIG PARTICIPANT RESOLUTION
========================================

For:

  script.TypeMultisig

the classifier provides PubKeys.

Do NOT set Output.Address.

Instead, resolve each pubkey through the same Core descriptor method:

  pkh(<pubkey>)

and populate:

  Output.ParticipantAddresses

parallel to:

  Output.PubKeys

Preserve duplicate pubkey entries in the chain.Output model.

Store already applies identity-set deduplication when persisting
output_participants.

If one participant cannot be resolved:

  return a decoding/resolution error

rather than silently shortening ParticipantAddresses and creating a shape
that Store will reject.


